Move the QCA8084 reset GPIO from the MDIO bus node to the chip's clock
controller node, where the binding (qcom,qca8k-nsscc.yaml) requires it.
On &mdio the MDIO core pulses the line for 10 us before the bus scan,
before the ETH0_50MHZ reference clock is enabled, and the nsscc driver,
which enables that clock and then holds the reset for 100 ms ("the
reference clock of QCA8k NSSCC needs to be enabled to make sure the GPIO
reset taking effect"), finds no GPIO and does nothing. The stock firmware
resets the chip with the reference clock running, and the Alta Labs
Route10 (same IPQ9574 + QCA8084) carries the same comment about a chip
left half-initialized when the reset sits on &mdio.
--- a/target/linux/qualcommbe/dts/ipq9574-be7000.dts
+++ b/target/linux/qualcommbe/dts/ipq9574-be7000.dts
@@ -615,11 +615,18 @@
 &mdio {
 	status = "okay";
 	clock-frequency = <6250000>;
-	reset-gpios = <&tlmm 60 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
 
+	/*
+	 * The QCA8084 reset lives here, not on &mdio: the nsscc driver
+	 * enables the ETH0_50MHZ reference clock and then holds the reset
+	 * for 100 ms, which is what makes the chip's internal reset take
+	 * effect. On &mdio the line is only pulsed for 10 us before the
+	 * bus scan, with the reference clock still off.
+	 */
 	qca8k_nsscc: clock-controller@18 {
 		compatible = "qcom,qca8084-nsscc";
 		reg = <0x18>;
+		reset-gpios = <&tlmm 60 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
 		clocks = <&cmn_pll ETH0_50MHZ_CLK>,
 			 <0>,
 			 <0>,
